{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1571516529,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0NDE5NDM5Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3767#issuecomment-544194396"},"message":"A nice example @kyleam !  Indeed, shouldn't be done \"by default\".\n\nSo we need to provide an option/convenience to be able to instruct `rerun` what to do in case of such cherry-picking problems. \n\nOne way would indeed be `interactive`.  May be #2092 could be a bit \"flipped inside out\" -- instead of `rerun` to somehow record its stage, and fail with instructions on what to do so it could pick up where it left of (similar to what `git` does), `rerun` could just open a new interactive shell (tty), with instructions to user to resolve all conflicts and `git add/rm` what needed, and just quit (Ctrl-d, or \"exit\") that shell.  Then `rerun` would continue with commit/save'ing (may be adding some note into the commit message on what conflicted) and proceed forward.\n\nBut I also see value in options for non-interactive resolution, such as in this case an option to `redo-removes` (or alike), which would in case of a \"conflict\" just `git rm -f` those files on users' behalf and continue.","files":null}]}